# Final Wiring Implementation Plan

This document explains exactly how the different modules of the Plagiarism Detection System will be wired together in `main.cpp` once everything is fully implemented.

## The Architecture Map

Right now, the modules are separated so they don't depend on each other unnecessarily. When the project is finished, `main.cpp` will act as the **Controller**. It will coordinate the data flow like this:

```mermaid
graph TD
    Main[main.cpp (Controller)] --> FM[FileManager]
    Main --> AN[Analyzer]
    Main --> UI[Raylib UI]

    FM -- "Reads .cpp files from disk" --> Doc[Document Structs]
    Doc -- "Raw text" --> AN
    AN -- "Calculates Jaccard Score" --> Score((Jaccard Score))
    Score -- "Passes score to draw" --> UI
```

## The Final Wiring in `main.cpp`

When we are ready to finish the project, `main.cpp` will look something like this. Notice how clean it is—the complex logic is hidden inside the modules.

```cpp
#include "FileManager.hpp"
#include "Analyzer.hpp"
#include "UI.hpp"
#include "Document.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // 1. Initialize our modules
    pd::FileManager fileManager;
    pd::Analyzer analyzer;
    pd::UI ui;

    // 2. Open the Raylib Window
    if (!ui.initialize(800, 900, "Plagiarism Detection System")) {
        return -1; // Failed to open window
    }

    // 3. Load files from the disk (Using FileManager)
    pd::Document doc1;
    doc1.path = "data/student1.cpp";
    doc1.rawText = fileManager.readFile(doc1.path);

    pd::Document doc2;
    doc2.path = "data/student2.cpp";
    doc2.rawText = fileManager.readFile(doc2.path);

    // 4. Analyze the code (Using Analyzer & Tokenizer)
    analyzer.preprocess(doc1); // Strips comments, tokenizes
    analyzer.preprocess(doc2);
    
    double similarityScore = analyzer.computeJaccard(doc1, doc2);

    // 5. The Main UI Loop (Using UI)
    while (!WindowShouldClose()) { // Raylib loop
        
        // Pass the score and document names to the UI to draw
        ui.render(doc1.path.filename().string(), 
                  doc2.path.filename().string(), 
                  similarityScore);
    }

    // 6. Cleanup
    ui.shutdown();
    return 0;
}
```

## Module Responsibilities During Wiring

To make sure there are **no errors** and nothing breaks, we must respect the boundaries of each module:

### 1. `FileManager`'s Job
It only talks to the hard drive. `main.cpp` asks it to read a file, and it returns a giant `std::string` of raw text. It does *not* know what the Analyzer or UI are.

### 2. `Analyzer`'s Job
It takes the raw text, strips the comments, breaks it into tokens, and does the Jaccard math. It returns a `double` (e.g., `0.75`). It does *not* know how to read files or draw to the screen.

### 3. `UI`'s Job
It takes the numbers and strings given to it by `main.cpp` and draws them using Raylib commands (`DrawText`, `DrawRectangle`, etc.). It does *not* do any math or read any files.

### 4. `main.cpp`'s Job
It is the only file that includes *all* the other modules. It acts as the bridge, passing data from the FileManager to the Analyzer, and passing the Analyzer's score to the UI.

## Next Steps for Development

To get to this final wired state, these are the remaining steps:
1. **Implement `FileManager.cpp`**: Write the code to actually read `.txt` or `.cpp` files from a folder.
2. **Implement `UI.cpp`**: Move the Raylib commands out of `main.cpp` and into the `UI::render()` function.
3. **Wire `main.cpp`**: Connect them together exactly as shown in the code block above.
